es \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|\\Omega|<|\\Gamma|\\), intersecting at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\nDenote by \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)); the points occur on the line in the order \\(C,M,N,D\\).\nLet \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nA,\\;E,\\;P,\\;F\\ \\text{are collinear}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. The common chord is perpendicular to the line of centres\n\nSince the two circles intersect, the line joining their centres is the perpendicular bisector of the common chord; therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C,D\\in MN\\), (7) also yields  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp CD .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lemma 1 – the altitude from \\(P\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\)\n\nThe circumcentre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); this bisector is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nHence the altitude from the vertex \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\)) is parallel to \\(AB\\) by (7).  \nSince the orthocentre \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes, this altitude passes through \\(H\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lemma 2 – the required tangent‑angle equality\n\nConsider the unique spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) that sends the circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto the circle \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBecause the centres satisfy \\(A M N\\) collinearity, this similarity maps the point \\(C\\) (the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\)) to the point \\(D\\) (the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nFor any point \\(X\\) on \\(\\Omega\\) its image \\(X'\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and the three points \\(A,X,X'\\) are collinear.  \nApplying this to \\(X=E\\) we obtain that its image is precisely \\(F\\) (the second intersection of the line \\(AE\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n\nSince a spiral similarity preserves oriented angles, we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle BFA .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) the points \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, whence the rays \\(FA\\) and \\(FE\\) coincide; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFA = \\angle BFE .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nCombining (10) and (11) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle BFE .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A,E,P\\) are collinear, the angle between the line \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(BE\\) equals \\(\\angle AEB\\):\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle AEB .\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus from (12) and (13) we obtain the crucial equality  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE\\;}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Tangency of the required line\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nBy Lemma 1, the altitude from \\(P\\) (which is parallel to \\(AB\\)) passes through \\(H\\); consequently \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) the same angle as \\(AP\\) does:\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell,BE)=\\angle (AP,BE).\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nUsing (14) we rewrite (15) as  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (\\ell,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThe tangent‑chord theorem states that a line is tangent to a circle at a point \\(F\\) iff the angle it forms with a chord through \\(F\\) equals the angle subtended by that chord in the opposite arc.  \nSince \\(\\ell\\) satisfies this condition for the chord \\(BE\\) of the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎"
