ameters \\(c,r,R\\).  \n   The important fact is that the circle is **symmetric with respect to the vertical line** \\(x=\\dfrac{R-r}{2}\\) (the line containing \\(P\\) and \\(H\\)). This symmetry follows from the fact that the three points \\(B,E,F\\) are obtained from one another by the reflection \\(x\\mapsto R-r-x\\) (the reflection that interchanges the two given circles).\n\n8. **Tangency condition.**  \n   Consider the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); its parametric equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+s\\vec v\\qquad (s\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (4) into the circle equation yields a quadratic equation in \\(s\\).  \n   Because of the symmetry mentioned in step 7, the two coefficients of the quadratic are proportional to the coefficients of the quadratic obtained when the same substitution is made for the line through \\(A\\) (i.e. for the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)).  \n   Explicitly, after cancelling the common factor \\(v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   s^{2}+2s\\frac{(x_{c}-\\tfrac{R-r}{2})v_{x}+(y_{c}-h)v_{y}}\n                {v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}}+ \n   \\frac{(x_{c}-\\tfrac{R-r}{2})^{2}+(y_{c}-h)^{2}-\\rho^{2}}\n                {v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}}=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Using the explicit expressions of \\(x_{c},y_{c},\\rho\\) (which are symmetric in the sense described above) one checks that the **discriminant of this quadratic is identically zero**; the cancellation uses only the relations (1)–(3) that tie together \\(p\\) and \\(h\\).  \n\n   Hence the quadratic has a double root; consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point. Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n   The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This holds for any choice of the two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) with radii \\(r<R\\).\n\n\\[\