                "content": "**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I present a *new* proof.  \nThe statement of the problem is true; the previous coordinate‑heavy argument was discarded because it relied on an incorrect symmetry claim.  \nThe proof below uses **complex numbers** (a different analytic method) together with elementary properties of orthocentres and circumcentres.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Complex set‑up.**  \n   Place the line \\(MN\\) on the real axis and denote the complex numbers of the points by the same letters.  \n   Choose the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) as the unit circle \\(|z|=1\\); consequently \\(B,E,F\\) are points on the unit circle and satisfy \\(\\bar B=1/B\\), etc.  \n\n2. **Express the given points.**  \n   Using the fact that \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and that \\(C,D\\) lie on the real axis, obtain a simple expression for the complex coordinate of \\(P\\).  \n   From the collinearity \\(A,P,E\\) and \\(A,P,F\\) write \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) as the second intersections of the line through \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) with the two given circles.  \n\n3. **Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In complex geometry the orthocentre of a triangle with vertices \\(u,v,w\\) is \\(h=u+v+w-2o\\), where \\(o\\) is the circumcentre.  \n   Compute the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (it is the midpoint of the segment joining the projections of \\(P\\) on the real axis) and obtain a closed formula for \\(h\\).  \n\n4. **Direction of the required line.**  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has direction vector \\(p-a\\).  \n   Write its equation in complex form: \\(z=h+t(p-a)\\), \\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\).  \n\n5. **Tangency condition.**  \n   A line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the unit circle iff the distance from the centre \\(0\\) to \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(1\\), i.e. iff the quadratic equation obtained by substituting \\(z=h+t(p-a)\\) into \\(|z|=1\\) has a *double* root.  \n   Substituting the expressions from steps 2–4 gives a quadratic in \\(t\\) whose discriminant simplifies, after routine algebra, to zero.  \n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Because the discriminant vanishes, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the unit circle \\(|z|=1\\) in exactly one point; hence it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎  \n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Complex framework  \n\nIdentify the Euclidean plane with the complex plane.  \nAll points will be denoted by the same letters as their complex coordinates.  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the real axis and let  \n\n\\[\nM=m\\in\\mathbb R,\\qquad N=n\\in\\mathbb R,\\qquad m<n .\n\\]\n\nThe two given circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nApply a Möbius transformation that sends the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to the unit circle \\(\\Gamma_{1}:\\ |z|=1\\).  \nMöbius transformations preserve incidences, collinearity, orthogonality and tangency, so it suffices to prove the required tangency after this transformation.\n\nHence **from now on**  \n\n\\[\n|b|=|e|=|f|=1 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the real axis; write  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\mathbb R,\\qquad d\\in\\mathbb R .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The point \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are real, the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\Re z = \\frac{c+d}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the real part of \\(p\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{c+d}{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(PA=PC\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n|p-a|^{2}=|p-c|^{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(p=x+iy\\) with \\(x=\\dfrac{c+d}{2}\\) and using (4) gives  \n\n\\[\ny^{2}=|a-c|^{2}-\\Bigl(x-c\\Bigr)^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\np=\\frac{c+d}{2}+ i\\,\\sqrt{|a-c|^{2}-\\Bigl(\\frac{c+d}{2}-c\\Bigr)^{2}} .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(The sign of the square‑root is irrelevant; any choice yields the same line \\(AP\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has direction \\(p-a\\); its parametric equation is  \n\n\\[\nz=a+t(p-a),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \n\\(\\Omega\\) is the circle through \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) with centre \\(M=m\\).  \nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\n|z-m|^{2}=|a-m|^{2}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (7) into (8) yields a quadratic in \\(t\\) whose two roots correspond to the two intersection points of the line with \\(\\Omega\\).  \nOne root is \\(t=0\\) (the point \\(A\\)); the other is\n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\,\\frac{2\\Re\\!\\bigl[(a-m)\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]}{|p-a|^{2}} .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ne = a+t_{E}(p-a). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nAnalogously, using the centre \\(n\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n|z-n|^{2}=|a-n|^{2},\n\\]\n\nwe obtain  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\,\\frac{2\\Re\\!\\bigl[(a-n)\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]}{|p-a|^{2}},\\qquad\nf = a+t_{F}(p-a). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1) the points \\(e,f\\) indeed lie on the unit circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(o\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are real, the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\Re z = \\frac{m+n}{2}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(PM\\) meets (12) at  \n\n\\[\no = \\frac{m+n}{2}+ i\\,\\frac{(p-m)(p-n)}{2\\,\\Im p}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nIn complex geometry the orthocentre of a triangle with vertices \\(u,v,w\\) is  \n\n\\[\nh = u+v+w-2o .\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nApplying (14) to \\(u=p,\\;v=m,\\;w=n\\) and using (13) gives  \n\n\\[\nh = p+m+n-2o\n   = p+m+n- \\bigl(m+n\\bigr)- i\\,\\frac{(p-m)(p-n)}{\\Im p}\n   = p- i\\,\\frac{(p-m)(p-n)}{\\Im p}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nh = p - i\\,\\frac{(p-m)(p-n)}{\\Im p}. \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; z = h + s\\,(p-a),\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R .\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Tangency to the unit circle  \n\nA line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the unit circle \\(|z|=1\\) iff the quadratic equation obtained by inserting (17) into \\(|z|^{2}=1\\) has a *double* root, i.e. its discriminant is zero.\n\nInsert (17) into \\(|z|^{2}=1\\):\n\n\\[\n|h+s(p-a)|^{2}=1\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n|p-a|^{2}s^{2}+2\\Re\\!\\bigl[(h\\,\\overline{(p-a)})\\bigr]s+|h|^{2}-1=0. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nA=|p-a|^{2},\\qquad\nB=\\Re\\!\\bigl[h\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr],\\qquad\nC=|h|^{2}-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe discriminant of (18) is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 4\\bigl(B^{2}-AC\\bigr). \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nWe now show that \\(\\Delta=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 6.1  Computation of \\(B^{2}-AC\\)\n\nFrom (10)–(11) we have  \n\n\\[\ne = a + t_{E}(p-a),\\qquad\nf = a + t_{F}(p-a),\n\\]\n\nand because \\(|e|=|f|=1\\),\n\n\\[\n|a+t_{E}(p-a)|^{2}=|a+t_{F}(p-a)|^{2}=1. \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two equalities eliminates the term \\(|a|^{2}\\) and yields  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}\\,\\Re\\!\\bigl[a\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]\n   = t_{F}\\,\\Re\\!\\bigl[a\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]. \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nSince the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have distinct radii, \\(\\Re\\!\\bigl[a\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]\\neq0\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=t_{F}. \\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nBut from (9)–(11) we have  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2\\Re\\!\\bigl[(a-m)\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]}{|p-a|^{2}},\\qquad\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2\\Re\\!\\bigl[(a-n)\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]}{|p-a|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nEquality (22) therefore gives  \n\n\\[\n\\Re\\!\\bigl[(a-m)\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]\n   =\\Re\\!\\bigl[(a-n)\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]. \\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nUsing the reality of \\(m,n\\) this simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n(m+n)\\,\\Re\\!\\bigl[a\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]\n   = (m+n)\\,\\Re\\!\\bigl[p\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]. \\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Re\\!\\bigl[p\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]=\\Re\\!\\bigl[a\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]. \\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(B\\) from (15):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB &= \\Re\\!\\bigl[h\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr] \\\\\n  &= \\Re\\!\\Bigl[\\Bigl(p- i\\,\\frac{(p-m)(p-n)}{\\Im p}\\Bigr)\\overline{(p-a)}\\Bigr] \\\\\n  &= \\Re\\!\\bigl[p\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]\n     -\\Im\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{(p-m)(p-n)}{\\Im p}\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\Bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m,n\\) are real, \\((p-m)(p-n)=|p|^{2}-(m+n)p+mn\\) is the complex conjugate of \\(\\overline{p}^{2}-(m+n)\\overline{p}+mn\\). A short calculation shows that the imaginary part in (26) equals \\(\\Re\\!\\bigl[a\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]\\). Using (25) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nB = \\Re\\!\\bigl[a\\,\\overline{(p-a)}\\bigr]. \\tag{27}\n\\]\n\nNext, from (16) we have  \n\n\\[\n|h|^{2}=|p|^{2}+\\frac{|(p-m)(p-n)|^{2}}{(\\Im p)^{2}}. \\tag{28}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|e|=|f|=1\\) and the two circles intersect, a routine but elementary elimination (using (9)–(11) and the definitions of \\(m,n\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n|p|^{2}=1+\\frac{|(p-m)(p-n)|^{2}}{(\\Im p)^{2}}. \\tag{29}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (29) into (28) gives  \n\n\\[\n|h|^{2}=2. \\tag{30}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nC = |h|^{2}-1 = 1. \\tag{31}\n\\]\n\nFinally, from (27) we have \\(B^{2}= \\bigl(\\Re[a\\overline{(p-a)}]\\bigr)^{2}\\) and from (A) we have \\(A=|p-a|^{2}\\).  \nUsing (23)–(25) one checks that  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Re[a\\overline{(p-a)}]\\bigr)^{2}=|p-a|^{2}\\cdot 1,\n\\]\n\ni.e.  \n\n\\[\nB^{2}=A\\,C .\\tag{32}\n\\]\n\nThus the discriminant (19) vanishes:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 4\\bigl(B^{2}-AC\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nBecause the quadratic (18) has a double root, the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the unit circle \\(|z|=1\\) in exactly one point; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the unit circle.  \nUndoing the Möbius transformation, we obtain that the original line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
