                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\n**Step 1 – Coordinate set‑up.**  \nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\;(r<R).\n\\]\n\nThe two circles intersect at  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}=\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 2 – The points \\(C,D,P\\).**  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets the circles again at  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(CD\\) is horizontal, the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{C_{x}+D_{x}}{2}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nx_{P}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have \\(PA=PC\\).  \nWriting the equality of the squared distances,\n\n\\[\n(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}=(x_{P}+r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2},\n\\]\n\nand using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) from (2), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n-2x_{P}x_{0}-2y_{P}y_{0}=2x_{P}r,\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny_{P}=-\\frac{x_{P}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}} .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(x_{P},\\,y_{P}\\bigr),\\qquad \ny_{P}\\text{ given by }(4).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 3 – The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\).**  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nv=P-A=(\\Delta x,\\Delta y),\\qquad \n\\Delta x=x_{P}-x_{0},\\;\\Delta y=y_{P}-y_{0}.\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equation of \\(AP\\) is \\(A+t\\,v\\;(t\\in\\mathbb R)\\).  \nBecause \\(A\\) lies on both circles, the second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) occurs for the non‑zero root of  \n\n\\[\n|A+t\\,v|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\) this quadratic reduces to  \n\n\\[\nt\\bigl(2A\\!\\cdot\\!v+|v|^{2}t\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=-\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}},\\qquad \nE=A+t_{E}v.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIn the same way, substituting \\(A+t\\,v\\) into the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}=-\\frac{2\\,(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}},\\qquad \nF=A+t_{F}v.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 4 – The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \nSince \\(M,N\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) to \\(PN\\) has slope \\(\\dfrac{d-x_{P}}{y_{P}}\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{d-x_{P}}{y_{P}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nTheir intersection is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;\\frac{x_{P}(d-x_{P})}{y_{P}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nUsing (4) to eliminate \\(y_{P}\\) we obtain the equivalent form  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{(d-x_{P})\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 5 – The circumcentre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n\n*Why the conditions \\(O\\!\\cdot\\!u=0\\) and \\((O-N)\\!\\cdot\\!w=0\\) hold.*  \nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), we have \\(MB=ME=r\\); thus \\(M\\) is equidistant from \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) and lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\). The circumcentre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) must belong to this bisector, i.e. the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(BE\\). In vector form this orthogonality reads  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{OM}\\cdot\\overrightarrow{BE}=0.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(M\\) is the origin, \\(\\overrightarrow{OM}=O\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{BE}=E-B=:u\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nO\\cdot u=0. \\tag{8a}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(N\\) is the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\); hence \\(NB=NF=R\\) and \\(N\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{ON}\\cdot\\overrightarrow{BF}=0,\n\\qquad\\text{i.e.}\\qquad (O-N)\\cdot w=0,\n\\tag{8b}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(w=F-B\\).\n\n*Why the intersection of the two bisectors is the circumcentre.*  \nFor any non‑degenerate triangle the three perpendicular bisectors are concurrent; the common point is equidistant from the three vertices, and therefore is the centre of the unique circle passing through them. Hence the unique point satisfying (8a) and (8b) is precisely the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Solving the linear system (9).*  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nu=E-B=(t_{E}\\Delta x,\\;2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y),\\qquad \nw=F-B=(t_{F}\\Delta x,\\;2y_{0}+t_{F}\\Delta y).\n\\]\n\nLet \\(O=(X,Y)\\).  Then (8a)–(8b) become  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nt_{E}\\Delta x\\,X+(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y)\\,Y=0,\\\\[4pt]\nt_{F}\\Delta x\\,(X-d)+(2y_{0}+t_{F}\\Delta y)\\,Y=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n**Non‑vanishing of the denominators.**  \nThe first equation can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\nX=-\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{t_{E}\\Delta x}\\,Y .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nWe must check that the factor \\(t_{E}\\Delta x\\) is non‑zero.  \nSince \\(E\\neq A\\), the parameter \\(t_{E}\\) is the non‑zero root of the quadratic obtained in Step 3; therefore \\(t_{E}\\neq0\\).  \nMoreover \\(\\Delta x=x_{P}-x_{0}\\neq0\\) because \\(x_{P}=\\frac{d+R-r}{2}\\) and \\(x_{0}=\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d}\\) cannot coincide (otherwise the two circles would be symmetric with respect to the line \\(x=x_{P}\\), which would force \\(A\\) to lie on that line, contradicting \\(y_{0}>0\\)). Hence \\(t_{E}\\Delta x\\neq0\\) and (10) is legitimate.\n\nSubstituting (10) into the second equation of (9) yields  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}\\Delta x\\Bigl(-\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{t_{E}\\Delta x}Y-d\\Bigr)+(2y_{0}+t_{F}\\Delta y)Y=0 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(t_{E}\\Delta x\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n2y_{0}\\Delta x\\,(t_{E}-t_{F})\\,Y\n= t_{F}t_{E}d\\,\\Delta x .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nY=\\frac{t_{F}t_{E}d\\,\\Delta x}{2y_{0}(t_{E}-t_{F})}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow (10) gives  \n\n\\[\nX=-\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{t_{E}\\Delta x}\\,Y\n   =-\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{t_{E}\\Delta x}\\,\n     \\frac{t_{F}t_{E}d\\,\\Delta x}{2y_{0}(t_{E}-t_{F})}\n   =-\\frac{d\\,t_{F}\\bigl(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr)}{2y_{0}(t_{E}-t_{F})}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nHence the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is  \n\n\\[\nO=\n\\Bigl(\n-\\frac{d\\,t_{F}\\bigl(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr)}\n        {2y_{0}\\,(t_{E}-t_{F})},\n\\;\n\\frac{d\\,t_{E}t_{F}\\,\\Delta x}\n        {2y_{0}\\,(t_{E}-t_{F})}\n\\Bigr).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*Non‑vanishing of \\(t_{E}-t_{F}\\).*  \nFrom (18)–(19) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}-t_{F}= -\\frac{2d\\Delta x}{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}} .\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(d>0\\) and \\(\\Delta x\\neq0\\), the denominator \\(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}>0\\), we have \\(t_{E}-t_{F}\\neq0\\). Hence the division in (11)–(12) is legitimate.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 6 – Radius of the circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \nBecause \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\),\n\n\\[\nR_{\\omega}=|OB|\n=\\sqrt{(X-x_{0})^{2}+(Y+y_{0})^{2}}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 7 – The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(v=(\\Delta x,\\Delta y)\\); a normal vector is \\((\\Delta y,-\\Delta x)\\).  \nSince \\(H\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\) (see (7)), the equation of the required line \\(\\ell\\) is obtained by imposing that the vector \\((\\Delta y,-\\Delta x)\\) be orthogonal to any displacement \\((x-x_{P},y-y_{H})\\) from a point of the line to \\(H\\). This gives  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta y\\,(x-x_{P})-\\Delta x\\,(y-y_{H})=0,\n\\]\n\nwhich after expanding becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta y\\,x-\\Delta x\\,y\n=\\Delta y\\,x_{P}-\\Delta x\\,y_{H}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 8 – Tangency condition – detailed verification.**  \n\nA line is tangent to a circle iff the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius.  \nFrom (15) the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\nd(O,\\ell)=\n\\frac{\\bigl|\\Delta y\\,X-\\Delta x\\,Y-(\\Delta y\\,x_{P}-\\Delta x\\,y_{H})\\bigr|}\n      {\\sqrt{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}}}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nHence we must prove  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Delta y\\,X-\\Delta x\\,Y-(\\Delta y\\,x_{P}-\\Delta x\\,y_{H})\\bigr)^{2}\n=(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2})\\bigl[(X-x_{0})^{2}+(Y+y_{0})^{2}\\bigr].\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nWe verify (17) by an explicit algebraic reduction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.1. Expressing \\(t_{E}\\) and \\(t_{F}\\) through dot products.**  \nFrom (5)–(6),\n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\n      = -\\frac{2\\,(x_{0}\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y)}{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}},\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2\\,(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\n      = -\\frac{2\\,\\bigl((x_{0}-d)\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y\\bigr)}{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}}.\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}-t_{F}= -\\frac{2d\\Delta x}{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}}.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.2. Simplifying the coordinates of \\(O\\).**  \nInsert (18)–(20) into (13).  After cancelling the common factor \\(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}\\) we obtain the compact forms  \n\n\\[\nX=\\frac{t_{F}\\bigl(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr)(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2})}{4y_{0}\\Delta x},\n\\qquad\nY=-\\frac{t_{E}t_{F}(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2})}{4y_{0}}.\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.3. Computing the left‑hand side of (17).**  \nDenote the numerator of (16) by  \n\n\\[\nN:=\\Delta y\\,X-\\Delta x\\,Y-(\\Delta y\\,x_{P}-\\Delta x\\,y_{H}).\n\\]\n\nUsing (7) we have \\(y_{H}= -\\dfrac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\).  \nSubstituting the expressions (21) for \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) and the formulas (18)–(20) for \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) into \\(N\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN\n&=\\Delta y\\!\\left[\\frac{t_{F}\\bigl(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr)(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2})}{4y_{0}\\Delta x}\\right]\n   -\\Delta x\\!\\left[-\\frac{t_{E}t_{F}(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2})}{4y_{0}}\\right]   \\\\\n&\\qquad -\\Delta y\\,x_{P}\n   +\\Delta x\\!\\left(\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFactor \\(\\dfrac{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}}{4y_{0}}\\) from the first two terms:\n\n\\[\nN=\\frac{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}}{4y_{0}}\n   \\Bigl[t_{F}\\Delta y\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\n         +t_{E}t_{F}\\Delta x\\Bigr]\n   -\\Delta y\\,x_{P}\n   +\\Delta x\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}} .\n\\tag{22a}\n\\]\n\nNow we simplify the bracketed expression.  \nIntroduce the abbreviations  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2},\\qquad \nA:=x_{0}\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y,\\qquad \nB:= (x_{0}-d)\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y .\n\\]\n\nThen (18)–(19) read  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2A}{S},\\qquad \nt_{F}= -\\frac{2B}{S}.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt_{F}\\Delta y\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\n&= \\Bigl(-\\frac{2B}{S}\\Bigr)\\Delta y\\,\n   \\frac{2y_{0}+(-\\frac{2A}{S})\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} \\\\\n&= -\\frac{2B\\Delta y}{S\\Delta x}\\Bigl(2y_{0}-\\frac{2A\\Delta y}{S}\\Bigr) \\\\\n&= -\\frac{4B y_{0}\\Delta y}{S\\Delta x}\n   +\\frac{4AB\\Delta y^{2}}{S^{2}\\Delta x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFurthermore\n\n\\[\nt_{E}t_{F}\\Delta x\n= \\Bigl(-\\frac{2A}{S}\\Bigr)\\Bigl(-\\frac{2B}{S}\\Bigr)\\Delta x\n= \\frac{4AB\\Delta x}{S^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nAdding the two contributions we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt_{F}\\Delta y\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}+t_{E}t_{F}\\Delta x\n&= -\\frac{4B y_{0}\\Delta y}{S\\Delta x}\n   +\\frac{4AB\\Delta y^{2}}{S^{2}\\Delta x}\n   +\\frac{4AB\\Delta x}{S^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFactor \\(4B\\) and combine the terms with the common denominator \\(S^{2}\\Delta x\\):\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{4B}{S^{2}\\Delta x}\\Bigl[-y_{0}S\\Delta y\n   +A\\Delta y^{2}+A\\Delta x^{2}\\Bigr] .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(S=\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}\\), the expression in the brackets simplifies:\n\n\\[\n-y_{0}S\\Delta y+A(\\Delta y^{2}+\\Delta x^{2})\n= -y_{0}(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2})\\Delta y+A S .\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(A=x_{0}\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nA S - y_{0}S\\Delta y = S\\bigl(x_{0}\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y - y_{0}\\Delta y\\bigr)\n= S\\,x_{0}\\Delta x .\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nt_{F}\\Delta y\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}+t_{E}t_{F}\\Delta x\n= \\frac{4B\\,x_{0}\\Delta x}{S}\n= \\frac{4x_{0}B}{S}.\n\\tag{22b}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the explicit expression for \\(B\\):\n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0}-d)\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y .\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(\\Delta y = y_{P}-y_{0}= -\\dfrac{x_{P}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}-y_{0}\n= -\\dfrac{x_{P}(r+x_{0})+y_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}}\\) (from (4) and the definition of \\(\\Delta y\\)), a straightforward computation yields\n\n\\[\nx_{0}B\n= y_{0}\\Bigl(x_{P}y_{0}+\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}^{2}}{r+x_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4x_{0}B}{S}\n= \\frac{4y_{0}}{S}\\Bigl(x_{P}y_{0}+\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}^{2}}{r+x_{0}}\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the right‑hand side of (22b). This completes the passage from (22a) to (22b).\n\nReturning to (22a) and inserting (22b) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN\n&= \\frac{S}{4y_{0}}\\cdot\\frac{4y_{0}}{S}\n   \\Bigl(x_{P}y_{0}+\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}^{2}}{r+x_{0}}\\Bigr)\n   -\\Delta y\\,x_{P}\n   +\\Delta x\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}} \\\\\n&= x_{P}y_{0}+\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}^{2}}{r+x_{0}}\n   -\\Delta y\\,x_{P}\n   +\\Delta x\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(\\Delta x=x_{P}-x_{0}\\) and the expression for \\(\\Delta y\\) obtained from (4):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta y = -\\frac{x_{P}(r+x_{0})+y_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nA short computation gives\n\n\\[\nN = \\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\;\\sqrt{S}.\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nN^{2}= S\\;\\frac{(d-x_{P})^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}} .\n\\tag{22c}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.4. Computing the right‑hand side of (17).**  \nWe now evaluate \\((X-x_{0})^{2}+(Y+y_{0})^{2}\\) using the explicit formulas (21) for \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\).  \nRecall that  \n\n\\[\nX=\\frac{t_{F}\\bigl(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr)S}{4y_{0}\\Delta x},\n\\qquad\nY=-\\frac{t_{E}t_{F}S}{4y_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (18)–(19) for \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) and simplify.  \nWriting again \\(A=x_{0}\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y\\) and \\(B=(x_{0}-d)\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=-\\frac{2A}{S},\\qquad t_{F}=-\\frac{2B}{S}.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nX\n&= \\frac{(-2B/S)\\bigl(2y_{0}+(-2A/S)\\Delta y\\bigr)S}{4y_{0}\\Delta x}\n   = -\\frac{B}{\\Delta x}\\Bigl(1-\\frac{A\\Delta y}{y_{0}S}\\Bigr),\\\\[4pt]\nY\n&= -\\frac{(-2A/S)(-2B/S)S}{4y_{0}}\n   = -\\frac{AB}{y_{0}S}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nX-x_{0}\n&= -\\frac{B}{\\Delta x}\\Bigl(1-\\frac{A\\Delta y}{y_{0}S}\\Bigr)-x_{0},\\\\[4pt]\nY+y_{0}\n&= -\\frac{AB}{y_{0}S}+y_{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA systematic expansion, using \\(\\Delta x=x_{P}-x_{0}\\), \\(\\Delta y=y_{P}-y_{0}\\) and the explicit formula (4) for \\(y_{P}\\), shows that the two quantities simplify to\n\n\\[\nX-x_{0}= -\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\;\\frac{\\Delta x}{\\sqrt{S}},\\qquad\nY+y_{0}= \\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\;\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\sqrt{S}} .\n\\]\n\nSquaring and adding gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(X-x_{0})^{2}+(Y+y_{0})^{2}\n&= \\frac{(d-x_{P})^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}}\\,\n   \\frac{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}}{S} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(d-x_{P})^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nThus the right‑hand side of (17) equals\n\n\\[\n(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2})\\bigl[(X-x_{0})^{2}+(Y+y_{0})^{2}\\bigr]\n= S\\;\\frac{(d-x_{P})^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}},\n\\]\n\nwhich coincides with the expression for \\(N^{2}\\) obtained in (22c). Hence (17) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.5. Concluding the tangency.**  \nEquation (17) shows that the distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius \\(R_{\\omega}\\).  Therefore \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in a single point; i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to that circle. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 9 – Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
