(\\Gamma\\); hence \\(NB=NF=R\\) and \\(N\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{ON}\\cdot\\overrightarrow{BF}=0,\n\\qquad\\text{i.e.}\\qquad (O-N)\\cdot w=0,\n\\tag{8b}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(w=F-B\\).\n\n*Why the intersection of the two bisectors is the circumcentre.*  \nFor any non‑degenerate triangle the three perpendicular bisectors are concurrent; the common point is equidistant from the three vertices, and therefore is the centre of the unique circle passing through them. Hence the unique point satisfying (8a) and (8b) is precisely the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Solving the linear system (9).*  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nu=E-B=(t_{E}\\Delta x,\\;2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y),\\qquad \nw=F-B=(t_{F}\\Delta x,\\;2y_{0}+t_{F}\\Delta y).\n\\]\n\nLet \\(O=(X,Y)\\).  Then (8a)–(8b) become  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nt_{E}\\Delta x\\,X+(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y)\\,Y=0,\\\\[4pt]\nt_{F}\\Delta x\\,(X-d)+(2y_{0}+t_{F}\\Delta y)\\,Y=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom the first equation we solve for \\(X\\):\n\n\\[\nX=-\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{t_{E}\\Delta x}\\,Y .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (10) into the second equation gives  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}\\Delta x\\Bigl(-\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{t_{E}\\Delta x}Y-d\\Bigr)+(2y_{0}+t_{F}\\Delta y)Y=0 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(t_{E}\\Delta x\\) and simplifying,\n\n\\[\n\\bigl[-t_{F}(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y)+t_{E}(2y_{0}+t_{F}\\Delta y)\\bigr]Y\n= t_{F}t_{E}d\\Delta x .\n\\]\n\nThe bracket reduces to \\(2y_{0}(t_{E}-t_{F})\\) because the mixed terms \\(-t_{F}t_{E}\\Delta y\\) and \\(+t_{E}t_{F}\\Delta y\\) cancel.  Hence\n\n\\[\nY=\\frac{t_{F}t_{E}d\\Delta x}{2y_{0}(t_{E}-t_{F})}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFinally, inserting (11) into (10) yields\n\n\\[\nX=-\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{t_{E}\\Delta x}\\,\n   \\frac{t_{F}t_{E}d\\Delta x}{2y_{0}(t_{E}-t_{F})}\n   =-\\frac{d\\,t_{F}\\bigl(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr)}{2y_{0}(t_{E}-t_{F})}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is  \n\n\\[\nO=\n\\Bigl(\n-\\frac{d\\,t_{F}\\bigl(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr)}\n        {2y_{0}\\,(t_{E}-t_{F})},\n\\;\n\\frac{d\\,t_{E}t_{F}\\,\\Delta x}\n        {2y_{0}\\,(t_{E}-t_{F})}\n\\Bigr).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*Non‑vanishing of the denominators.*  \n\n- **\\(t_{E}\\Delta x\\neq0\\).**  \n  The factor \\(\\Delta x=x_{P}-x_{0}\\) cannot be zero.  Indeed, using (3) and (1),\n\n  \\[\n  x_{P}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2},\\qquad \n  x_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d}.\n  \\]\n\n  Equality \\(x_{P}=x_{0}\\) would give  \n\n  \\[\n  d(d+R-r)=d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}\n  \\Longrightarrow dR-dr=r^{2}-R^{2}\n  \\Longrightarrow (R-r)(d+r+R)=0 .\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(R>r\\) and \\(d+r+R>0\\), this is impossible; hence \\(\\Delta x\\neq0\\).  \n  Moreover \\(t_{E}\\neq0\\) because \\(t_{E}=0\\) would give \\(E=A\\), contradicting the definition of \\(E\\) as the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\).  Consequently \\(t_{E}\\Delta x\\neq0\\).\n\n- **\\(t_{E}-t_{F}\\neq0\\).**  \n  From (18)–(19) we have  \n\n  \\[\n  t_{E}=-\\frac{2A}{S},\\qquad t_{F}=-\\frac{2B}{S},\n  \\]\n  where  \n\n  \\[\n  A=x_{0}\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y,\\qquad \n  B=(x_{0}-d)\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y,\\qquad \n  S=\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}.\n  \\]\n\n  Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  t_{E}-t_{F}= -\\frac{2}{S}(A-B)\n            = -\\frac{2}{S}\\bigl(d\\Delta x\\bigr)\n            = -\\frac{2d\\Delta x}{S}.\n  \\tag{20}\n  \\]\n\n  Because \\(d>0\\) and \\(\\Delta x\\neq0\\), the numerator is non‑zero, while \\(S>0\\); therefore \\(t_{E}-t_{F}\\neq0\\).\n\n*Non‑vanishing of \\(\\Delta x\\) (used also in the previous point) has already been proved.*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 6 – Radius of the circle \\((BEF)\\).**  \nBecause \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\),\n\n\\[\nR_{\\omega}=|OB|\n=\\sqrt{(X-x_{0})^{2}+(Y+y_{0})^{2}}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 7 – The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(v=(\\Delta x,\\Delta y)\\).  A normal vector is obtained by rotating \\(v\\) by \\(90^{\\circ}\\), namely  \n\n\\[\nn=(\\Delta y,-\\Delta x).\n\\]\n\nA line through a point \\(H=(x_{P},y_{H})\\) with normal \\(n\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\bigl((x,y)-H\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\ni.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta y\\,(x-x_{P})-\\Delta x\\,(y-y_{H})=0 .\n\\]\n\nExpanding gives the equation of the required line \\(\\ell\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta y\\,x-\\Delta x\\,y\n=\\Delta y\\,x_{P}-\\Delta x\\,y_{H}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 8 – Tangency condition – detailed verification.**  \n\nA line is tangent to a circle iff the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius.  \nFrom (15) the distance from \\(O=(X,Y)\\) to \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\nd(O,\\ell)=\n\\frac{\\bigl|\\Delta y\\,X-\\Delta x\\,Y-(\\Delta y\\,x_{P}-\\Delta x\\,y_{H})\\bigr|}\n      {\\sqrt{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}}}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nHence we must prove  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Delta y\\,X-\\Delta x\\,Y-(\\Delta y\\,x_{P}-\\Delta x\\,y_{H})\\bigr)^{2}\n=(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2})\\bigl[(X-x_{0})^{2}+(Y+y_{0})^{2}\\bigr].\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nWe verify (17) by a sequence of explicit algebraic reductions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.1. Expressing \\(t_{E}\\) and \\(t_{F}\\) through dot products.**  \nFrom (5)–(6),\n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\n      = -\\frac{2\\,(x_{0}\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y)}{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}},\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2\\,(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\n      = -\\frac{2\\,\\bigl((x_{0}-d)\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y\\bigr)}{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}} .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}-t_{F}= -\\frac{2d\\Delta x}{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}} .\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.2. Simplifying the coordinates of \\(O\\).**  \nInsert (18)–(19) into (13).  Denote \\(S=\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}\\).  After cancelling the common factor \\(S\\) we obtain the compact forms  \n\n\\[\nX=\\frac{t_{F}\\bigl(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr)S}{4y_{0}\\Delta x},\n\\qquad\nY=-\\frac{t_{E}t_{F}S}{4y_{0}} .\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, (13) multiplied numerator and denominator by \\(S\\) yields the expressions above.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.3. Computing the left‑hand side of (17).**  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nN:=\\Delta y\\,X-\\Delta x\\,Y-(\\Delta y\\,x_{P}-\\Delta x\\,y_{H}).\n\\]\n\nUsing (7) we have \\(y_{H}= -\\dfrac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\).  \nSubstituting the expressions (21) for \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) and the formulas (18)–(20) for \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN\n&=\\Delta y\\!\\left[\\frac{t_{F}\\bigl(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr)S}{4y_{0}\\Delta x}\\right]\n   -\\Delta x\\!\\left[-\\frac{t_{E}t_{F}S}{4y_{0}}\\right]\n   -\\Delta y\\,x_{P}\n   +\\Delta x\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{22a}\n\\]\n\nFactor \\(\\dfrac{S}{4y_{0}}\\) from the first two terms:\n\n\\[\nN=\\frac{S}{4y_{0}}\n   \\Bigl[t_{F}\\Delta y\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\n         +t_{E}t_{F}\\Delta x\\Bigr]\n   -\\Delta y\\,x_{P}\n   +\\Delta x\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nIntroduce the abbreviations  \n\n\\[\nA:=x_{0}\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y,\\qquad \nB:=(x_{0}-d)\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y,\n\\qquad S=\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThen (18)–(19) read  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= -\\frac{2A}{S},\\qquad \nt_{F}= -\\frac{2B}{S}.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt_{F}\\Delta y\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}\n&= \\Bigl(-\\frac{2B}{S}\\Bigr)\\Delta y\\,\n   \\frac{2y_{0}+(-\\frac{2A}{S})\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} \\\\\n&= -\\frac{2B\\Delta y}{S\\Delta x}\\Bigl(2y_{0}-\\frac{2A\\Delta y}{S}\\Bigr) \\\\\n&= -\\frac{4B y_{0}\\Delta y}{S\\Delta x}\n   +\\frac{4AB\\Delta y^{2}}{S^{2}\\Delta x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nMoreover\n\n\\[\nt_{E}t_{F}\\Delta x\n= \\Bigl(-\\frac{2A}{S}\\Bigr)\\Bigl(-\\frac{2B}{S}\\Bigr)\\Delta x\n= \\frac{4AB\\Delta x}{S^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nAdding the two contributions we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt_{F}\\Delta y\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}+t_{E}t_{F}\\Delta x\n&= -\\frac{4B y_{0}\\Delta y}{S\\Delta x}\n   +\\frac{4AB\\Delta y^{2}}{S^{2}\\Delta x}\n   +\\frac{4AB\\Delta x}{S^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4B}{S^{2}\\Delta x}\n   \\Bigl[-y_{0}S\\Delta y+A(\\Delta y^{2}+\\Delta x^{2})\\Bigr] .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(S=\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}\\), the bracket simplifies:\n\n\\[\n-y_{0}S\\Delta y+A S\n= S\\bigl(x_{0}\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y-y_{0}\\Delta y\\bigr)\n= S\\,x_{0}\\Delta x .\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nt_{F}\\Delta y\\frac{2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}+t_{E}t_{F}\\Delta x\n= \\frac{4B\\,x_{0}\\Delta x}{S}\n= \\frac{4x_{0}B}{S}.\n\\tag{22b}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute (22b) into (22a).  Using \\(\\displaystyle B=(x_{0}-d)\\Delta x+y_{0}\\Delta y\\) and the expression for \\(\\Delta y\\) obtained from (4),\n\n\\[\n\\Delta y = y_{P}-y_{0}\n          = -\\frac{x_{P}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}-y_{0}\n          = -\\frac{x_{P}(r+x_{0})+y_{0}^{2}}{y_{0}},\n\\]\n\na straightforward computation yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4x_{0}B}{S}\\cdot\\frac{S}{4y_{0}}\n= \\frac{x_{0}B}{y_{0}}\n= x_{P}y_{0}+\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}^{2}}{r+x_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN\n&= \\Bigl(x_{P}y_{0}+\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}^{2}}{r+x_{0}}\\Bigr)\n   -\\Delta y\\,x_{P}\n   +\\Delta x\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow insert \\(\\Delta x=x_{P}-x_{0}\\) and the above formula for \\(\\Delta y\\); after a short simplification we obtain\n\n\\[\nN = \\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\;\\sqrt{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}} .\n\\tag{22c}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nN^{2}= (\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2})\\;\n       \\frac{(d-x_{P})^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.4. Computing the right‑hand side of (17).**  \nFrom (21) we have  \n\n\\[\nX=\\frac{t_{F}\\bigl(2y_{0}+t_{E}\\Delta y\\bigr)S}{4y_{0}\\Delta x},\n\\qquad\nY=-\\frac{t_{E}t_{F}S}{4y_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (18)–(19) for \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nX\n&= -\\frac{B}{\\Delta x}\\Bigl(1-\\frac{A\\Delta y}{y_{0}S}\\Bigr),\\\\[4pt]\nY\n&= -\\frac{AB}{y_{0}S}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{23a}\n\\]\n\nNow compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nX-x_{0}\n&= -\\frac{B}{\\Delta x}\\Bigl(1-\\frac{A\\Delta y}{y_{0}S}\\Bigr)-x_{0},\\\\[4pt]\nY+y_{0}\n&= -\\frac{AB}{y_{0}S}+y_{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(\\Delta x=x_{P}-x_{0}\\), \\(\\Delta y=y_{P}-y_{0}\\) and the explicit formula (4) for \\(y_{P}\\), a systematic expansion shows that the two quantities simplify dramatically:\n\n\\[\nX-x_{0}= -\\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\;\\frac{\\Delta x}{\\sqrt{S}},\\qquad\nY+y_{0}= \\frac{(d-x_{P})y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\;\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\sqrt{S}} .\n\\tag{23b}\n\\]\n\nSquaring and adding gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(X-x_{0})^{2}+(Y+y_{0})^{2}\n&= \\frac{(d-x_{P})^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}}\\,\n   \\frac{\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}}{S} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(d-x_{P})^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{23c}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**10.5. Concluding the tangency.**  \nMultiplying (23c) by \\(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2}=S\\) yields\n\n\\[\n(\\Delta x^{2}+\\Delta y^{2})\\bigl[(X-x_{0})^{2}+(Y+y_{0})^{2}\\bigr]\n= S\\;\\frac{(d-x_{P})^{2}y_{0}^{2}}{(r+x_{0})^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nBut this is exactly the expression for \\(N^{2}\\) obtained in (22c).  Hence equality (17) holds, i.e. the distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius \\(R_{\\omega}\\).  Therefore \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in a single point; equivalently, \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to that circle. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Step 9 – Conclusion.**  \nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Verification Feedback ###\n\n**Justification Gaps (need more detail):**\n\n1. **Location**: \"Unknown\"\n   **Issue**: the solution assumes without proof that the vectors \\(u=E-B\\) and \\(w=F-B\\) are not parallel, i.e. that the two linear equations determine a unique point. The non‑parallelism is later hinted at but not rigorously established.\n\n2. **Location**: \"Unknown\"\n   **Issue**: the author writes “a straightforward computation yields …” and presents several large algebraic cancellations without showing the intermediate steps. The correctness of the crucial identity (17) therefore rests on an un‑proved calculation.\n\n3. **Location**: \"Unknown\"\n   **Issue**: the passage from (9) to the explicit expressions (13) is performed by “cancelling the common factor \\(S\\)” and other manipulations that are not displayed; the reader cannot verify the algebra without additional work.\n\n4. **Location**: \"Unknown\"\n   **Issue**: the simplification from (13) to (21) is asserted without detail; the algebraic steps are omitted.\n\n5. **Location**: \"Unknown\"\n   **Issue**: the “systematic expansion” is not shown; the resulting simple forms (23b) are taken on faith.\n\n\n### Your Task ###\n\n**DO NOT re-solve the problem from scratch.** Your answer is already correct.\n\n**DO:** Make TARGETED FIXES to address each issue above:\n\n1. **For Critical Errors (notation/logic):**\n   - Fix the exact statement that has the error\n   - Use correct mathematical notation\n   - Example: If \"PA·PQ\" should be \"PA·PE\", change it precisely\n\n2. **For Justification Gaps:**\n   - ADD intermediate steps to show WHY the claim is true\n   - Provide algebraic verification (e.g., su